}
```

To ensure long-term fiscal sustainability and maximize cost-benefit ROI across both municipalities, I propose shifting $90 million from San Francisco to New York, reflecting the greater aggregate economic density and existing infrastructure leverage in NYC which allows for more efficient per-unit climate resilience spending. Conversely, San Francisco requires a targeted reduction to correct the over-allocation relative to its smaller population base, focusing funds there only on high-impact, low-cost measures like green roof retrofits rather than broad expansion. This rebalancing preserves the total $300 million pool while optimizing resource deployment where economic efficiency is highest and avoiding fiscal strain in areas with lower marginal returns.

