The current disparity favors New York disproportionately given its lower historical climate vulnerability compared to San Francisco's exposure to seismic and wildfire risks; reallocating funds toward the Bay Area ensures higher cost-benefit ROI for immediate resilience infrastructure while maintaining fiscal sustainability across both metro areas. To balance economic efficiency and risk mitigation, I propose shifting $43.75 million from New York to San Francisco, creating a unified strategy that prioritizes high-impact spending in the more vulnerable jurisdiction without exceeding the $300 million total cap.

